  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Robustness Requirements**\n\nMy initial assessment reveals impressive robustness. Now, I\'m focusing on implementation details. Specifically, I\'m evaluating integrating Google Drive OAuth, either server-side or client-side. Hold on, I\'m re-reading the user\'s request, to ensure I\'m focused on the most critical challenge!\n\n\n**Designing Full-Stack Architecture**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in designing the full-stack architecture. My current focus is on integrating an Express server with the existing Vite frontend. I\'m exploring the `package.json` scripts to facilitate this, including understanding the optimal conversion process.\n\n\n**Defining Script Configurations**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the `package.json` script updates are correct for full-stack integration; this is crucial for the Express and Vite connection. I\'m now outlining the OAuth authorization and upload flow. The user interaction begins with a click, triggering a URL fetch, redirecting them to Google\'s consent screen with appropriate scopes.\n\n\n**Analyzing OAuth Integration**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the OAuth flow implementation. Specifically, I\'m examining how to handle Google\'s redirect back after user consent, focusing on capturing the `code` and exchanging it for tokens. I\'m leaning heavily towards the `postMessage` strategy, after reviewing the example from the skill. The advantages of keeping the `accessToken` and user info in-memory on the client side, seems superior to session-based approach.\n\n\n**Refining Upload Workflow**\n\nI\'ve got it! The client efficiently manages `OAUTH_AUTH_SUCCESS` by storing tokens in React state. Now, I\'m focusing on the upload process, considering both drag-and-drop and the custom form. I realized I must account for the initial CSV paths, needing to create the exact folder structure in Google Drive. My approach involves checking and creating folders if they don\'t exist. Let\'s create a root folder like \"Trade2Turkey Logistics\" to mirror the requested hierarchy, making uploads intuitive!\n\n\n**Optimizing Library Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the dependencies to keep the project clean. I realized I can use standard `fetch` or `axios` requests with the OAuth token for Google APIs, avoiding bulky SDKs and their potential initialization issues. The Google Drive API seems straightforward to implement, specifically for folder creation. I\'ve confirmed I can POST to `/drive/v3/files` with the `Authorization` header and a JSON body specifying the folder name and `mimeType`.\n\n\n**Implementing Server-Side Upload**\n\nI\'ve decided on a server-side approach for the file upload. My rationale centers on cleaner handling of multipart form data and enhanced security. The plan involves creating an Express endpoint at `/api/drive/upload`. The server will receive the file, parse it, and then proxy the file stream to Google\'s Drive API, ensuring a secure and efficient upload process. I\'ll need to remember to update my client side implementation to upload to this new endpoint.\n\n\n**Defining Environment Variables**\n\nI\'m now focusing on setting up Google OAuth 2.0. I\'ve determined I need `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` environment variables. I\'ll add them to `.env.example` to guide user configuration, and ensure the user understands their importance, especially in cases where AI Studio\'s pre-provisioned setup fails. The goal is a seamless credential setup, whether using the studio\'s flow or the user\'s custom credentials.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I am going to check the files inside `/src/components` to make sure we know exactly what components exist."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 622009633
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
