  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing OAuth Failure**\n\nOkay, so I\'m stuck on this OAuth failure, which still presents as a `BadRequestException` and `No location information provided`. I\'m trying to figure out the root cause. It\'s unclear why it\'s missing location data. I\'m going to re-examine the request parameters and see where the data gets set.\n\n\n**Investigating OAuth Configuration**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential OAuth configuration issues. The project ID might still be empty, which could be the root cause, or the `set_up_oauth` tool might expect a specific ID/number. It\'s also possible side-channel headers are not being passed correctly. I\'m exploring whether the user\'s project already has Google OAuth set up. I\'m going to search the codebase for relevant configuration parameters. So far I have found a relevant section in `server.ts`.\n\n\n**Examining CLIENT_ID Source**\n\nI\'ve traced the `CLIENT_ID` source. The application code prioritizes user-configured credentials from `.env` or Secrets, specifically `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` or `CLIENT_ID`. I\'ve confirmed in `server.ts` that if these are present, they are used for the OAuth redirect URL, bypassing any other setup. I\'m now exploring if a failed `set_up_oauth` call during development causes deployment issues or if deployment failure is related to another invalid argument the deployment builder encountered. The deployment logs provide a critical clue.\n\n\n**Debugging Build Environment**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the build environment, suspecting a possible invalid argument issue. I\'m carefully reviewing `package.json`, specifically the `build`, `dev`, and `start` scripts. I\'m investigating the potential of `esbuild` or `tsx` integration within the deployment builder. I am curious whether there\'s some platform incompatibility. It seems likely the platform deployment builder might have expectations for specific application types, particularly given the metadata\'s server-side focus.\n\n\n**Exploring Server Direct Execution**\n\nI\'m now investigating running the server directly in production. My application uses an Express backend (`server.ts`) to handle Google OAuth and file uploads. I\'ve realized `/server.ts` is in the root and that built assets go to `dist/`. Examining `package.json`, I\'m questioning whether `--packages=external` in the build script is causing deployment issues and am now exploring whether it\'s safe to directly run `server.ts` with `tsx`, rather than compiling with `esbuild`.\n\n\n**Confirming Execution Method**\n\nI\'ve just had a major breakthrough and verified the server execution method. The instructions explicitly state that for custom servers, like mine with `server.ts`, Node can directly run the TypeScript, eliminating the need for compilation. This directly relates to a section in \"Full-Stack Apps (Custom Server)\" and \"Full-Stack Applications (Express + Vite).\" This is a key finding and removes the concern of compilation.\n\n\n**Validating Deployment Arguments**\n\nI\'m now investigating \"Request contains an invalid argument\" errors in the deployment log. It appears the error is a configuration issue in `package.json` or related files. I\'ll need to check the arguments passed to the deployment builder. I\'m going to look at `metadata.json` to verify the deployment\'s metadata, and examine all files, including `package.json`, `vite.config.ts`, and `server.ts`. I think there is an invalid argument passed due to the configuration.\n\n\n**Validating Request Permissions**\n\nI\'m now focused on the empty `requestFramePermissions` in the metadata. I\'m exploring if this field requires specific keys like \"camera,\" \"microphone,\" or \"geolocation.\" I\'m investigating if the platform expects any other value for this field. I\'m also double-checking if there is another invalid key in `metadata.json`. I\'m going to search for any hidden or system configuration file to see if there is any other value. I\'ve confirmed `\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY